`lowerExpressionStatement` (`src/compiler/ts/lower/statement.ts:219-244`) accepts `++`/`--`, `=` and the compound assignments, and reports everything else as `Unsupported expression statement` (TS8099). So

```ts
function store(i: u32) { dst[i] = 1.; }
@compute @workgroup_size(64)
export function main(@builtin("global_invocation_id") gid: vec3<u32>) {
  store(gid.x);
}
```

is rejected: any function whose result is discarded cannot be spelled. The same hole is what keeps `workgroupBarrier()`, `textureStore(...)` and `atomicAdd(...)` from ever landing, since each is a call whose value is unused. #24 adds a seven-line special case for the bare `discard` identifier only, so the general gap remains after it.

Fix: lower a `CallExpression` statement to the IR's call statement (the EDSL has a spelling for a discarded call; use the same node), for user functions and intrinsics alike, with a diagnostic when the callee returns a value that is silently dropped only if the language wants that rule (WGSL allows discarding a call's value with `_ = f();` and requires it for non-void; decide and document).

